10.1  Wall clock and monotonic clock: measuring time right

TL;DR
  - The wall clock says what time it is. It can jump: NTP corrects it, an admin
    sets it, a VM resumes. Never measure durations with it.
  - The monotonic clock only moves forward, from an arbitrary start (boot): made
    for durations and timeouts.
  - A battery-powered hardware clock (RTC) keeps time while the machine is off;
    the kernel reads it at boot, then counts with a CPU counter.

      date                       # wall clock, in your time zone
      cat /proc/uptime           # seconds since boot: monotonic
      timedatectl                # wall clock, RTC, zone, NTP status

On your machine
  Zone      Europe/Paris
  RTC       the hardware clock keeps UTC
  Source    tsc (also available: hpet, acpi_pm)
  Sync      systemd-timesyncd, synchronised
  Booted    2026-10-11 06:26 UTC

Pitfalls
  - end - start with wall-clock times can be negative or huge across an NTP
    step: use time.monotonic() (Python), performance.now() (JS), Instant (Rust);
    Go's time.Since already does.
  - The monotonic clock stops during suspend; CLOCK_BOOTTIME doesn't.
  - Comparing timestamps from two machines assumes both clocks are right: their
    difference is only as good as NTP (10.2).

Deep dive: 2 questions. Read them with idk 10.1 --why

Related
  Commands  date, timedatectl
  Paths     /sys/devices/system/clocksource/
  See also  idk 10.2 Keeping the clock right: NTP
  See also  idk 1.4 Interrupts: how devices get attention
